<Base title="Reviewing a contract — Career Central">
  <div class="prose">
  <h1>Reading a contract before you sign</h1>
  <p>
    An offer email is marketing; the contract attached to it is the actual
    deal, and most of the terms that matter later are in the clauses nobody
    reads twice.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you accept</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Read the whole document, not just the offer email.</li>
    <li>Check the pay against the relevant award or the Fair Work Ombudsman's pay tools, so you know if it's at, above, or below the legal minimum for the role.</li>
    <li>Confirm start date, probation length, and notice periods explicitly — ask if anything's unclear rather than guessing.</li>
    <li>Ask for a few days to review — a reasonable employer will give you this; being asked to sign on the spot is itself a signal.</li>
    <li>Get anything agreed verbally (a start-date change, a role adjustment) added to the written contract — verbal promises that aren't in the document are hard to rely on later.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What to check</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Employment type</strong> — casual, part-time, full-time, or fixed-term (see <a href="/guides/job-types/">job types</a>); this changes what leave and notice you're entitled to.</li>
    <li><strong>Pay</strong> — the actual rate, whether it's before or after tax, and whether super is on top of it or included in a "package" figure.</li>
    <li><strong>Hours and location</strong> — the hours you're actually committing to, and whether the role can require relocation or on-site attendance beyond what was discussed verbally.</li>
    <li><strong>Probation period</strong> — how long, and what (if anything) changes about notice or entitlements during it.</li>
    <li><strong>Notice period</strong> — how much notice either side has to give to end it.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Restraint of trade / non-compete clauses</strong> — what you're
      restricted from doing after you leave, for how long, and in what
      geographic area. A restriction that's broad, long, or vague is worth
      questioning — courts don't enforce ones that go further than protecting
      a genuine business interest, but you don't want to find that out the
      hard way.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Confidentiality and IP clauses</strong> — especially anything claiming ownership of things you build or write outside work hours.</li>
    <li><strong>Any clawback clause</strong> — training costs or bonds you'd owe back if you leave within a set period.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Red flags</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>An unpaid "trial" longer than the time it genuinely takes to demonstrate a skill — extended unpaid trial shifts are a known underpayment pattern, not a normal part of hiring.</li>
    <li>Being asked to get an ABN for what's described as a normal job with set hours and supervision — see the sham-contracting note in <a href="/guides/getting-set-up/">the ABN guide</a>.</li>
    <li>Vague duties, vague hours, or a start date with no written contract yet — get it in writing before you resign from anything else or make other commitments.</li>
    <li>Pressure to sign immediately, with no time to read it properly.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    This page is general orientation, not legal advice on your specific offer
    — for that, especially anything with a restraint-of-trade, IP, or
    clawback clause that looks non-standard, <a href="/coaching">book a query</a> and
    have someone actually read it with you.
  </p>
  </div>
</Base>
